# AGENTS.md

Guidance for coding agents on this repo. Engineering conventions (stack, structure,
commit rules) live in `CLAUDE.md` / `CONTRIBUTING.md` — read those for anything not
covered here.

## Pull request workflow (all agents)

When CREATING a pull request: fill every section of `.github/pull_request_template.md`
with real content — actual verification results, real trade-offs with reasoning. The AI
reviewers treat stated trade-offs as intentional and verify Verification claims.

After OPENING a pull request, immediately and without being asked: fire the Codex
review trigger (`gh pr comment <N> --body "@codex review"` — skip only if a Codex
review/ack already exists for this head), then follow `.github/ai-review-loop.md` to
convergence. It is autonomous by design — verified fixes, every thread replied to AND
resolved, re-triggers; only its critical escalations stop for a human, and it NEVER
merges.

At the START of a session in this repo: check your open PRs in THIS repository only
(`gh search prs --author "@me" --state open --repo <owner>/<repo>` — other repos'
sweeps belong to their own sessions/crons) and run the loop's sweep, restricted to
this repo, if anything is actionable. Quiet when idle.

## Code Review Rules

<!-- Keep this exact heading: it is the section Codex reads for repo-specific review
     rules (openai/codex#25738) AND the signal the merge-gate uses to detect that this
     repo runs Codex reviews. Renaming it disables both. -->

You are the **adversarial reviewer** on this repo. If a separate conventions reviewer
runs here (Claude, via the `code-review` GitHub Action), it owns style, naming,
conventions, and accessibility — do NOT duplicate it. Your job is the opposite lens:
**assume the change is wrong and try to prove it.**

Flag only real correctness / robustness / security risks — **P0 (breaks or is unsafe)**
and **P1 (likely bug or risky)**:

- **Logic bugs** — off-by-one, inverted conditions, wrong operator, mishandled
  empty / null / undefined, incorrect assumptions about inputs.
- **Edge cases the change doesn't handle** — boundary values, empty collections,
  concurrent/duplicate calls, very large inputs, unexpected data shapes.
- **Error handling** — swallowed errors, unhandled rejections, missing validation at
  trust boundaries, partial failures that leave inconsistent state.
- **Security** — injection, unsafe HTML, secrets in code, missing auth/authz on server
  routes, SSRF, path traversal, ReDoS, unvalidated redirects.
- **Regressions / breaking changes** — silent behavior changes, broken public
  contracts, data-loss paths, migrations without safe rollback.

<!-- Add repo-specific traps here, e.g. framework footguns, build-vs-runtime rules. -->

Rules of engagement:

- **Only P0 / P1.** Skip nitpicks and anything linters or the conventions reviewer
  already catch. Signal over noise.
- For each finding give: **what breaks**, the **concrete trigger** (inputs/state), and
  the **fix**.
- **Don't invent issues.** If you genuinely can't break it, say so in one line — a clean
  adversarial pass is a valid, useful result.
